\newlabel{fig:bounce-pr}{{6.2b}{43}{Precision-recall curves.\relax }{figure.caption.30}{}}
\newlabel{sub@fig:bounce-pr}{{b}{43}{Precision-recall curves.\relax }{figure.caption.30}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Bounce-detection comparison on the game7 test split. (a)\nobreakspace  {}Event $F_1$ as the matching tolerance $k$ widens from $0$ (exact frame) to $7$ frames. At the exact frame the temporal network is the most precise (0.717 against the gradient-boosted model's 0.660 and the heuristic's 0.326), but from $k = 1$ onward the gradient-boosted model leads, and both learned models plateau by $k = 1$ while the heuristic continues to gain as the tolerance loosens. (b)\nobreakspace  {}The precision-recall curves traced by sweeping the decode threshold, whose areas are the average precisions of Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:bounce-results}: the temporal network encloses the largest area (AP 0.972), the gradient-boosted model the next (0.874), and the heuristic far less (0.394).\relax }}{43}{figure.caption.30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bounce-comparison}{{6.2}{43}{Bounce-detection comparison on the game7 test split. (a)~Event $F_1$ as the matching tolerance $k$ widens from $0$ (exact frame) to $7$ frames. At the exact frame the temporal network is the most precise (0.717 against the gradient-boosted model's 0.660 and the heuristic's 0.326), but from $k = 1$ onward the gradient-boosted model leads, and both learned models plateau by $k = 1$ while the heuristic continues to gain as the tolerance loosens. (b)~The precision-recall curves traced by sweeping the decode threshold, whose areas are the average precisions of Table~\ref {tab:bounce-results}: the temporal network encloses the largest area (AP 0.972), the gradient-boosted model the next (0.874), and the heuristic far less (0.394).\relax }{figure.caption.30}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces What each model fires on, bucketing every predicted bounce by whether the nearest ground-truth event within the tolerance window is a true bounce, a racket hit, or neither. The heuristic mistakes 31 racket hits for bounces, which is the majority of its false positives, because a hit produces a velocity reversal and acceleration spike much like a bounce. Both learned models, trained with hits as hard negatives, mistake only a single hit each, so almost all of their few false positives are firings on neither event.\relax }}{44}{figure.caption.31}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bounce-confusion}{{6.3}{44}{What each model fires on, bucketing every predicted bounce by whether the nearest ground-truth event within the tolerance window is a true bounce, a racket hit, or neither. The heuristic mistakes 31 racket hits for bounces, which is the majority of its false positives, because a hit produces a velocity reversal and acceleration spike much like a bounce. Both learned models, trained with hits as hard negatives, mistake only a single hit each, so almost all of their few false positives are firings on neither event.\relax }{figure.caption.31}{}}
